    (N'00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000000002ef', N'00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000000002b1', N'00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000000002b2', N'Postmortem: Claims Processing (JP): intermittent 502 errors and pod restarts', N'# Postmortem: Claims Processing (JP): intermittent 502 errors and pod restarts

## Summary

Parts of Claims Processing (JP) intermittently fail because a service keeps running out of memory and restarting. Users see occasional errors when documents are rendered. A temporary memory increase and restart stabilise it; the underlying memory leak needs a code fix.

## Impact

Claims Processing (JP) was degraded for 29 minutes. Claims Processing (JP) saw intermittent 502 errors while pods of claims-document-renderer restarted repeatedly. Kubernetes reports containers OOMKilled and backing off, and restart counts increased.

## Timeline

| Time | Event |
| --- | --- |
| 2026-11-03 04:33 UTC | Incident INC004090137 opened in ServiceNow |
| 2026-11-03 04:34 UTC | Keigan started investigating |
| 2026-11-03 04:36 UTC | Evidence collected: 24 items, 7 strong |
| 2026-11-03 04:37 UTC | Root cause analysis ready: cause identified (confidence 82%) |
| 2026-11-03 04:53 UTC | Responder confirmed the proposed fix was applied |
| 2026-11-03 05:02 UTC | Incident resolved |

## Root cause

Containers of claims-document-renderer reach their 1 GiB memory limit and are killed by the kernel [E4]. Memory grows by roughly 300 MB per hour, which indicates a leak in the document renderer rather than a one-off spike. While pods restart the gateway has no healthy backend and returns 502.

## Contributing factors

- Existing alerts did not describe the failing component clearly enough to shorten diagnosis.
- The relevant limit or dependency was not covered by a capacity or readiness check.

## Detection

The incident was raised in ServiceNow and picked up by Keigan, which started the investigation immediately and collected evidence within minutes.

## Response

Responders followed the investigation''s proposed fix. Total time to restore service: 29 minutes.

## What went well

- Keigan assembled evidence and a ranked root cause hypothesis before responders joined.
- Stakeholders received bilingual updates early.

## What could be improved

- Detection depended on a user-visible symptom; an earlier signal would shorten time to detect.
- The fix steps were manual and should be rehearsed.

## Action items
| Action | Owner role | Due | Type | Priority |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Fix the image buffer leak in the document renderer and add a soak test | lead | 2026-11-24 | prevent | top |
| Alert when container memory exceeds 80 percent of its limit | manager | 2026-11-10 | detect | normal |
| Set pod disruption budgets and a second replica to absorb restarts | infra | 2026-11-17 | mitigate | normal |

## Lessons learned

